# a clone of the Unix command `cat`, written in steel

# declares the program uses the file at the first command argument for the full lifetime
# `take` locks a system resource (file) for the entire lifetime of the program when it
# launches and crashes if it doesn't work
file = take in.1 | out
# in.0 is stdin, in.1 and later are arguments

# `=` binds a value to a name, creating an immutable variable lasting the full scope
# variables are specific data types that can be written to using |>
# no variables are used in this example, but you'd use `my_var = cool_system | var`
# the term var creates a writable region of memory from input

# the program is a set of declarations, which bind values to names (variables)
# the part that actually runs is the variable `main`, which executes the system
# bound to it (you could also have a value, but that would be pointless)
# `|` pipes the output of the last system into the input of the next like shell

# this is a system, which has an input type (accessed as `in`, read only), a body (which does process),
# and an output type (accessed as `out`, write only), written as in_type -> body -> out_type
# all systems taking input from `in' are triggered at once, but that's only one here, more on that in grep
main = lst[str] ->
    in | as_str | out
-> str

# you probably figured it out but # is a comment
